---
import Lede from './Lede.astro';
/**
 * EDUCATIONAL LEADER — slice R3.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Educational Leader":
 *
 *   Entrance      A — HAIRLINE HEAD. Kicker + subtitle outdented to
 *                 columns 1-3, capped by a hairline rule. This is the
 *                 page's FIRST outdented kicker, and the staircase it
 *                 makes is the whole contrast against About's drop-cap
 *                 entrance directly above it. About opens with a cap
 *                 and no kicker and no rule; this opens with a rule and
 *                 a kicker and no cap.
 *   Column window body 4-11 (band 1)
 *   Image         NONE, and deliberately so. About into Educational
 *                 Leader is the page's longest text-only stretch, ~175
 *                 words. A full-width photographic band was considered
 *                 as a break and REJECTED: all client photography is
 *                 vertical and a wide crop would mean cropping a tall
 *                 portrait into a shape it does not have. The mitigation
 *                 is typographic — the display-scale opening statements
 *                 on both sections carry it. Do not solve this with an
 *                 image.
 *   Rhythm        110px. About is 96px. Sections MUST NOT share a
 *                 padding token.
 *   Margin holds  the district link.
 *   Length        full; coda set apart.
 *
 * NO HEIGHT CONSTRAINT. No min-height, no svh floor. CONTEXT.md's R1.4
 * standing rule: the Cover is the ONLY section on this page with one.
 * This section is content-height.
 *
 * COPY is set verbatim from COPY.md Part 1 -> Educational Leader.
 * Five strings: kicker, subtitle, opening statement, two body
 * paragraphs, closing line. Nothing reworded, nothing added, and
 * nothing restored from the Appendix.
 *
 * THE DISTRICT EMAIL IS NOT PUBLISHED AND DOES NOT APPEAR IN THIS
 * FILE, including in a comment. It is her public-school-district
 * employer address; publishing it on a commercial consulting site is
 * an appearance-of-conflict and district-IT-policy problem. COPY.md
 * records the decision as final. The district WEBSITE link is retained
 * — that is public credential information.
 *
 * NO grid-column OR grid-row IS SET IN THIS FILE. Six collapse-aware
 * primitives carry every placement — .win-edu-head, -lede, -body,
 * -rule, -close, -margin — and each one's behaviour in all four bands
 * (>=1280 / 1024-1279 / 751-1023 / <=750) is written out in full above
 * the implementation in global.css, before the implementation.
 *
 * NO ROW IS DECLARED EITHER, and that is load-bearing. See the
 * "WHY THE LINK NEVER SHARES A BAND WITH THE LEDE'S LAST LINE" note in
 * global.css: the link clears the lede because grid seats it in a later
 * ROW, not because anything counted the lede's lines. There is no
 * swept pixel boundary and no line-count lookup in this component.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH, so no treatment layer and no blend to protect; the
 * section carries no transform of any kind either way.
 *
 * GOLD: ZERO. Both budgets are spent and this slice adds to neither.
 * The 3 saturated type accents (Legacy tagline, About drop cap, pull
 * quote) are untouched, and this section adds 0 of the 8 hairline-gold
 * rule contexts — BOTH of its rules are Smoked Slate at 0.38, the same
 * treatment as the Cover's foot rule.
 */
---

<section class="sheet edu" id="sec-education">
  <!--
    SOURCE ORDER IS STRUCTURAL, not cosmetic. head -> lede -> body ->
    margin -> rule -> closing is what makes grid's sparse cursor seat
    the district link in the BODY's row rather than the lede's, which
    is the whole mechanism by which it clears the lede with no line
    count. Reordering these siblings changes the composition. See
    global.css.

    It also gives the right stacked reading order below 750: kicker,
    subtitle, lede, body, district link, rule, closing line — the coda
    stays the last word of the section, and the link reads as the
    credential aside it is.
  -->
  <div class="win-edu-head edu-head">
    <!-- The head's hairline. A CHILD of the head rather than a grid
         item of its own, so it spans exactly the head's columns in
         every band without a second primitive to keep in step.
         `border: 0` + explicit height because Tailwind's preflight
         styles <hr> as a zero-height element with a top border. -->
    <hr class="edu-head-rule" />

    <!--
      THE <h2> IS THE KICKER. CONTEXT.md's locked heading structure:
      each section's <h2> is the element whose text names the section,
      and that string must equal the folio label. Verified against
      src/data/sections.ts line 29 — { id: 'sec-education', n: '03',
      label: 'Educational Leader' } — not assumed. The outdent moves
      where the heading sits; it does not change what it says.
    -->
    <h2 class="edu-kicker">Educational Leader</h2>
    <p class="edu-subtitle">Alternative Education High School Principal</p>
  </div>

  <!-- R18.1 — THE LEDE LINE MASK. The island nests INSIDE this
  paragraph; it does not replace it. The paragraph is the grid item
  and carries this section's placement, and Astro wraps a hydrated
  island in a real astro-island element — so an island mounted in
  the paragraph's place would become the grid item itself and move a
  left edge, which is the one thing this round may not do. Classes,
  placement and box are untouched. Only the text node moves into the
  island, and the island renders that same string verbatim until it
  has measured the rendered line boxes. Copy is COPY.md's. -->
  <p class="win-edu-lede edu-lede"><Lede text={"Education should not require students to fit into systems. Strong systems evolve to fit the\u00a0students."} /></p>

  <div class="win-edu-body edu-body">
    <p>For more than two decades, Dr.&nbsp;McClain has built her career around students whose potential does not show up in traditional measures.</p>
    <p>Her work in customized programming, alternative education, and student engagement creates pathways for students navigating complex academic and personal circumstances, built on innovation, collaboration, and unwavering advocacy.</p>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE LIVE MARGIN. Authored here — directly after the body and before
    the rule — so auto-placement seats it beside the body. Moving it to
    the end of the section would drop it into the closing line's row and
    empty the margin for the section's whole upper two thirds, which is
    the void condition the R-shell pass flagged and this slice exists to
    test against real content.
  -->
  <div class="win-edu-margin edu-margin">
    <p class="edu-margin-label">District</p>
    <a class="edu-link" href="https://www.ucvts.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ucvts.org<span class="vh"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>
  </div>

  <!-- The 38% rule. Its own grid item and its own primitive, because it
       sits on the BODY's left edge while the closing line below steps
       back out to the LEDE's — the two cannot share a placement. -->
  <hr class="win-edu-rule edu-rule" />

  <p class="win-edu-close edu-coda">Meet students where they are, without losing sight of where they can go.</p>
</section>

<style>
  /* CONTEXT.md's per-section padding table gives Educational Leader a
     flat 110 and About directly above it 96. Sections MUST NOT share a
     padding token, so the value is declared here and nowhere else —
     there is deliberately no --vr-* token in this rule.

     SYMMETRIC, unlike About's. About carries an asymmetric top
     (max(96px, 20svh + 12px)) for one reason only: it follows the
     Cover, which is 80svh, so the distance from the fold down to
     About's lede scales with viewport height and a fixed value cannot
     satisfy it. This section follows a CONTENT-HEIGHT neighbour, so
     that argument does not apply and a flat 110 is correct on both
     edges. Copying About's svh term here would be cargo-culting a fix
     for a problem this section does not have. */
  .edu{
    padding-block: 110px;
  }

  /* ---- THE HEAD — entrance A ------------------------------------
     Outdented to columns 1-3 and top-aligned in its row, so the rule
     sits on the section's top content edge and the kicker hangs to the
     LEFT of the lede rather than above it. That is the staircase. */

  /* Smoked Slate at 0.38 — the same hairline treatment as the Cover's
     foot rule, so the page has one rule, not two. NOT GOLD: the 8
     hairline-gold rule contexts are spent and CONTEXT.md routes every
     new rule to Slate. */
  .edu-head-rule{
    width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    margin: 0 0 var(--s2);
    border: 0;
    background: var(--slate);
    opacity: 0.38;
  }

  /* The kicker is an <h2>; the browser's heading margins and weight
     are reset here rather than inherited. Treatment matches the
     global .kicker primitive — 10.5px Montserrat, 0.26em tracking,
     uppercase, Smoked Slate — with the optical half-tracking pullback
     so the K's stem lands on the column line rather than the tracking
     space after it. */
  .edu-kicker{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: -0.13em;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    line-height: var(--baseline);
    letter-spacing: 0.26em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate);
  }

  /* The deck. SENTENCE CASE, deliberately: the kicker above it is
     uppercase and letterspaced, so setting the subtitle the same way
     would give the head two identical-looking lines and no hierarchy.
     Case is the differentiator, not size alone. Set verbatim from
     COPY.md. */
  .edu-subtitle{
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.012em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 1 of 3, REUSED FROM R2 RATHER THAN REINVENTED. Same face,
     same four axes via --fx-lede, same weight, same clamp, same
     line-height, same letterfit as About's lede — which in turn came
     from PillarPremier per CONTEXT.md's lede rule. Measured at 1440:
     3.8vw = 54.72px, which is 3.22x the 17px body and inside the
     3-4x target band.

     NO max-width. Measure is controlled by the column span and by
     nothing else. */
  .edu-lede{
    /* LEDE -> BODY. 0.75 of the lede's OWN line-height, via the `lh`
       unit, so it re-resolves from the type rather than from a width:
       the lede is clamp(52px, 3.8vw, 64px) at line-height 1.18, so the
       gap is 0.75 x 64.57 = 48.4px at 1440 and 0.75 x 61.4 = 46.0px at
       390, tracking the lede at every width with no breakpoint.

       Was clamp(44px, 4.4vw, 72px) = 63.4px at 1440. Tightened so the
       body reads as issuing from the lede rather than as the next of
       four evenly spaced blocks. Under one full lede line, so the two
       are closer to each other than either is to anything else. */
    margin: 0 0 0.75lh;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);    /* was the token's clamp restated; reads the token so the lede cap reaches it */
    line-height: 1.18;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    /* balance, not pretty — the right tool for a 2-4 line block of
       display type. Progressive enhancement: an engine without it
       wraps normally and the lede is merely unbalanced, never
       broken. The body keeps `pretty`. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3. --bio-size / --bio-lh, the page's body tokens,
     unchanged. NO max-width — span only. */
  .edu-body{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
  }

  .edu-body p{ margin: 0; }
  .edu-body p + p{ margin-top: 1.5em; }

  /* ---- THE LIVE MARGIN — the district link ----------------------
     BASELINE-SNAPPED, and the arithmetic CANCELS rather than being
     swept. CONTEXT.md: margin content snaps to body baselines, not to
     container tops.

     A first baseline sits at half-leading plus ascent, which reduces
     to  B = lh/2 + (ascent - descent)/2 x font-size.  Giving this
     block the BODY's own line-height makes the lh/2 term identical on
     both sides, so the whole expression collapses to the difference in
     font size alone:

         B_body - B_margin = (asc - desc)/2 x (17px - 10.5px)

     Montserrat's ascent - descent is 0.717 (published hhea 968/-251,
     and R2 cross-checked it at 0.716 from rendered ink), so the
     constant is 0.3585 and the offset is 0.3585 x 6.5px = 2.33px.

     Written with `1em` for the second term so it re-resolves off this
     block's own font-size, and with var(--bio-size) for the first, so
     a change to either tier moves the snap automatically. NOTHING here
     is a swept pixel boundary and no line count appears in it. */
  .edu-margin{
    font-size: 10.5px;
    line-height: calc(var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    margin-top: calc(0.3585 * (var(--bio-size) - 1em));
  }

  /* THE SNAP APPLIES WHEREVER THE MARGIN IS A COLUMN, WHICH IS NOW
     EVERY WIDTH FROM 751 UP. The previous version needed three
     overrides because the margin was a column, then inline, then a
     column again; deleting the 1024-1279 suppression collapsed that
     to one contiguous range, so two of the three are gone:

       >= 1280    column at 10-12  -> baseline snap (the rule above)
       1024-1279  column at 11-12  -> baseline snap, same rule
       751-1023   column at 7-8    -> baseline snap, same rule
       <= 750     inline           -> rhythm gap, below

     Below 750 the block falls inline under the body and a 2.33px
     baseline correction would read as a fourth line of the paragraph
     rather than as a marginal note, so it takes ordinary rhythm
     instead. --s4, because it has to read as a block change and not
     as a paragraph break. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .edu-margin{ margin-top: var(--s4); }
  }

  /* Slate at 0.82, not a lighter knock-down. Measured in R1: Slate on
     Porcelain is 7.54:1 at full strength; at 0.78 the composite falls
     to 4.45:1, under the floor. 0.82 composites to 4.90:1 and passes. */
  .edu-margin-label{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: -0.11em;           /* half of 0.22em tracking */
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--slate-label);    /* was Slate at 0.82; see the token */
  }

  /* Lowercase, so the URL reads as a URL. Underline offset rather than
     a border so the descenders in "g" are not struck through.
     Transform and opacity only — never transition-all. */
  .edu-link{
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 2px;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.4;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(71, 68, 64, 0.42);
    transition:
      transform 260ms var(--ease),
      opacity   260ms var(--ease);
  }

  .edu-link:hover{ transform: translateY(-1px); opacity: 0.7; }
  .edu-link:active{ transform: translateY(0); opacity: 1; }
  .edu-link:focus-visible{
    outline: 1px solid var(--obsidian);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-color: var(--obsidian);
  }

  /* ---- THE 38% RULE ---------------------------------------------
     Sits on the BODY's left edge. The closing line below it steps back
     out one column to the LEDE's edge, so the rule visibly overhangs
     the coda's left — that offset is what marks the coda as set apart
     rather than as a fourth paragraph.

     Same Slate hairline as the head's rule. Zero gold. */
  .edu-rule{
    width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    /* BODY -> RULE -> CODA, both derived from the BODY's line-height
       (var(--bio-size) x var(--bio-lh) = 30.6px), which is the reading
       rhythm the paragraphs above already sit on:

         body -> rule   2.5 body lines = 76.5px   (separates groups)
         rule -> coda   0.75 body line = 22.95px  (binds the lid on)

       A 3.3:1 ratio. The rule was previously 80.6px above and 40px
       below, which at 2:1 still read as a third free-floating element
       between two blocks. It now sits with the coda: the gap under it
       is the smallest in the section and the gap above it is the
       largest, so the eye groups rule+coda and reads the space above
       them as the section's one internal division.

       Both terms are multiples of a token, not pixels measured at one
       width. --bio-size is a fixed 17px at every band by design, so
       these hold across all four without a breakpoint — the reading
       rhythm does not change when the grid does. */
    margin:
      calc(2.5 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh)) 0
      calc(0.75 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    border: 0;
    background: var(--slate);
    opacity: 0.38;
  }

  /* ---- CODA ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 2 of 3, REUSED FROM R2. Fraunces at opsz 96 / SOFT 35 /
     wght 400 — all four axes stated, none left to Fraunces' defaults
     of wght 900 / WONK 1 / SOFT 100. These are NOT --fx-coda (opsz 48
     / SOFT 45 / wght 360); the Phase B optical system is preserved
     untouched and this tier states its own coordinates locally, the
     same way About's coda and the Cover's .legacy do.

     Measured at 1440: 2.2vw = 31.68px, which is 1.86x the 17px body
     and 0.58x the lede — clearly a third tier, subordinate to the
     lede and distinct from the body. */
  .edu-coda{
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BAND 4 · <= 750 -------------------------------------------
     One stacked column. The primitives in global.css already return
     every block to 1 / -1; what is left here is the two things a span
     cannot express.

     THE RULES DO NOT BECOME FULL-WIDTH DIVIDERS. Both hairlines are
     editorial marks, not separators, and a 1 / -1 rule across a phone
     measure reads as a horizontal line breaking the page in half. The
     head's rule keeps a fixed short length and the coda's rule keeps
     the same ~38% proportion it holds in every other band. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .edu-head-rule{ width: 96px; }
    .edu-rule{ width: 38%; }
  }

  /* ---- STACKED HEAD · <= 1023 -------------------------------------
     Below 1024 the head stacks above the lede (global.css band 3 puts
     both in column 1), and the grid has no row gap, so the subtitle
     ran straight into the lede. The head takes the same one-body-line
     margin Premier, Athletic and Charging give theirs, so kicker and
     subtitle read as one unit and the lede as the next. 1024 and up,
     beside the lede, is untouched. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px){
    .edu-head{ margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh)); }
  }

  /* ---- PHONE SECTION SPACING · <= 750 ------------------------------
     Phone boundaries land between --s5 and --s7, ranked as on desktop
     (largest: Index > Charging, About > Educational Leader, Educational
     Leader > Premier, Everyday Legends > Athletic, Premier > Everyday
     Legends; smallest: Cover > About). Above 750 nothing changes. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .edu{ padding-block: var(--vr-tight); }
  }
</style>
